{"type":"message","id":"992350b1","parentId":"2d6b7f31","timestamp":"2026-05-18T07:31:35.073Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Bitcoin Selloff Below $77K as Macro Pressure and 'Sell in May' Fears Build\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"bitcoin selloff\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Bitcoin Slides Under $77,000 as Oil Shock and Treasury Yields Hit Risk Assets\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Google News\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-under-usd77-000-as-oil-shock-and-treasury-yields-hit-risk-ass\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/sell-in-may-historical-pattern-could-see-btc-back-at-33k-analyst?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\",\n    \"https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMixAFBVV95cUxQajIxZExWVjJnMXVteHdZVGVZZFNzbzlvZXVNSFFLaWR2Mm5HekNyVmxrUXpTX0lMMnJfMkJmV1FGdlcwN0U0YVF3U3ZUU2x0ZGhnSG1icE9aM0VSQkJVQldVYnpMN1I1YlhIUXVZTUlTd2NZN2h6VjZieUx5LTFwV0FtRVZQREFCVEZFWTYtU182TFZDTERFcmcySVZqRF9GalRBbXJTR0h2WVVnaHdJa2E2a0dNZ1dURnlpbVpxWERLZkI1?oc=5\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"CoinTelegraph said Bitcoin was trading around $76,900 at the time of writing, down 5.6% over the past seven days.\",\n    \"The 'sell in May' debate is being driven by comparisons to earlier cycle drawdowns: Bitcoin fell from nearly $10,000 to about $7,000 in May 2018, and from about $40,000 to $28,500 in May 2022 before sliding further to $20,000 in June.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph quoted analyst Jeff Ko saying the calendar alone did not cause prior crashes; specific shocks such as the Mt. Gox aftermath, China ICO crackdowns, Fed tightening, and the Terra/FTX collapses were the real drivers.\",\n    \"Jeff Ko said he does not expect a repeat of the 70% to 80% drawdowns from past cycles because spot ETFs, corporate treasury adoption, and the CLARITY Act have broadened the buyer base.\",\n    \"Ko said a move toward the mid-$60k or high-$50k range could be defensible under a macro shock or a significant ETF outflow cascade, but a return to $33,000 would likely require something systemic to break.\",\n    \"Michaël van de Poppe said the current price action does not scream for new lows, but he flagged the $76,000 area as a key support level; losing it could open the door to a deeper decline.\",\n    \"CoinDesk’s feed framed the broader move as Bitcoin sliding under $77,000 as an oil shock and rising Treasury yields pressured risk assets.\",\n    \"Google News surfaced multiple corroborating headlines around the same move, including CoinDesk’s 'Why is bitcoin down? BTC slides to $78,000, SOL, DOGE, XRP down 5%' and Bloomberg’s 'Bitcoin (BTC) Hits Two-Week Low as Crypto Liquidations Top Half a Billion.'\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"CoinDesk RSS headline and summary: 'Bitcoin slides under $77,000 as oil shock and Treasury yields hit risk assets.' The feed summary said long-term holders are still sitting tight and exchange balances remain near six-year lows, according to Binance Research data, but underwater short-term holders leave BTC vulnerable to more downside. CoinDesk also highlighted another related market item: 'Crypto traders betting on a rally lose $563 million in liquidations. Ether and bitcoin suffer the most,' with the summary noting that ether and bitcoin led liquidations as prices dropped on macroeconomic concerns. CoinDesk’s RSS stream for today also included 'Why is bitcoin down? BTC slides to $78,000, SOL, DOGE, XRP down 5%' in Google News aggregation, reinforcing that this was a broad market selloff rather than a single-asset event.\\n\\nCoinTelegraph article text: Crypto analysts are divided over whether markets will see a major Bitcoin sell-off in May, a pattern that has emerged in the last two bear markets during US mid-term election years. In May 2018, Bitcoin crashed from nearly $10,000 to about $7,000 by the end of the month. It happened again in May 2022, when Bitcoin fell nearly 30% from about $40,000 to $28,500 before falling further in June to $20,000. With 2026 also a bear market year coinciding with a US mid-term election, there are concerns it could happen again. Crypto analyst Merlijn Enkelaar said, 'The most brutal pattern in Bitcoin history. Nobody wants to hear this. But the pattern is perfect. Mid-term election years. Bitcoin dumps. Every time.' Enkelaar said a similar move could see Bitcoin prices collapse to $33,000 despite the advancement of key legislation, the CLARITY Act, positive crypto sentiment from the Trump administration and potential trade deals between the US and China. Joao Wedson, founder and CEO of Alphractal, also said there would be a higher probability of a new capitulation phase if Bitcoin remains under $78,000, with bears 'showing signs of strength.' Bitcoin was trading at about $76,900 at the time of writing, down 5.6% over the past seven days.\\n\\nCoinTelegraph analysis continued: Jeff Ko, chief analyst at the CoinEx exchange, told Cointelegraph that midterm election years have coincided with major Bitcoin bear markets, 'so some traders may be tempted to frame 2026 as another “sell in May” setup.' However, behind that historical seasonality were more concrete macro drivers, such as the Mt. Gox aftermath, China’s ICO crackdown, Fed tightening and the Terra/FTX collapses. 'The calendar didn’t cause those drawdowns — specific shocks did.' Ko said he doesn’t expect BTC to repeat the 70% to 80% drawdowns seen in past cycles because the market structure has fundamentally changed. 'Spot ETFs, corporate treasury adoption, and the CLARITY Act moving through Congress have meaningfully broadened and institutionalized the buyer base compared with past cycles,' he added. 'In my view, a move toward the mid-$60k or high-$50k range could be defensible under a macro shock or a significant ETF outflow cascade. But a move back to $33k would likely require something genuinely systemic to break, rather than simply a repeat of historical seasonality.' MN Fund founder Michaël van de Poppe was also bullish, saying the current Bitcoin price action 'doesn’t shout for new lows' but is 'consolidating after a run of 40%.' However, an important support level that is currently preventing a larger decline is the $76,000 area, he cautioned. 'If that level is lost, I would assume that the markets will see a further downward fall towards lower boundaries.'\\n\\nGoogle News aggregation corroboration: one headline from Yahoo Finance read 'Harvard Dumps Its Ethereum and Bitcoin ETF Investment.' Another from CoinDesk repeated the broader move, 'Why is bitcoin down? BTC slides to $78,000, SOL, DOGE, XRP down 5%.' TradingView carried 'Crypto crash: why are Bitcoin Cash, LUNC, Pi Network, WLFI going down?' Crypto Briefing had a related headline, 'Iran launches Hormuz Safe, a Bitcoin-settled maritime insurance platform for Persian Gulf shipping.' The Block surfaced 'US crypto ATM operator Bitcoin Depot files for Chapter 11 to wind down business.' Bloomberg’s title in the feed said 'Bitcoin (BTC) Hits Two-Week Low as Crypto Liquidations Top Half a Billion.'\\n\\nTaken together, the live feeds point to a single dominant event today: a market-wide Bitcoin pullback into the $76k-$78k range, with liquidations, oil-related macro pressure, and weak risk sentiment driving the move. Analysts are debating whether this is just a seasonal 'sell in May' scare or the start of a deeper correction, but the immediate facts are that Bitcoin has slipped below a key support band, crypto liquidations have surged, and multiple outlets are framing the same downside move as the day’s central crypto story.\",\n  \"aggregated_raw_content_note\": \"All facts above are drawn from today's RSS items and the readable CoinTelegraph article text; CoinDesk article pages were rate-limited, so their feed summaries and Google News corroboration were used instead.\"\n}\nCHART_COIN: bitcoin"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4-mini","usage":{"input":2433,"output":2421,"cacheRead":29824,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":34678,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1779089481364,"responseId":"chatcmpl-Dgmje5IBRYhLHo8m0cL6UliJLKgNQ"}}
{"type":"message","id":"d8c34295","parentId":"992350b1","timestamp":"2026-05-18T07:31:55.083Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[Mon 2026-05-18 07:31 UTC] Follow your SOUL to cross-reference multiple RSS feeds, find the single biggest news event, and extract deep facts. Return the aggregated Single-Topic JSON."}],"timestamp":1779089515080}}
{"type":"message","id":"9979fac8","parentId":"d8c34295","timestamp":"2026-05-18T07:32:06.410Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Bitcoin Selloff Below $77K as Macro Pressure and 'Sell in May' Fears Build\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"bitcoin selloff\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Bitcoin Slides Under $77,000 as Oil Shock and Treasury Yields Hit Risk Assets\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Google News\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-under-usd77-000-as-oil-shock-and-treasury-yields-hit-risk-ass\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/sell-in-may-historical-pattern-could-see-btc-back-at-33k-analyst?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\",\n    \"https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMixAFBVV95cUxQajIxZExWVjJnMXVteHdZVGVZZFNzbzlvZXVNSFFLaWR2Mm5HekNyVmxrUXpTX0lMMnJfMkJmV1FGdlcwN0U0YVF3U3ZUU2x0ZGhnSG1icE9aM0VSQkJVQldVYnpMN1I1YlhIUXVZTUlTd2NZN2h6VjZieUx5LTFwV0FtRVZQREFCVEZFWTYtU182TFZDTERFcmcySVZqRF9GalRBbXJTR0h2WVVnaHdJa2E2a0dNZ1dURnlpbVpxWERLZkI1?oc=5\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"CoinTelegraph said Bitcoin was trading around $76,900 at the time of writing, down 5.6% over the past seven days.\",\n    \"The 'sell in May' debate is being driven by comparisons to earlier cycle drawdowns: Bitcoin fell from nearly $10,000 to about $7,000 in May 2018, and from about $40,000 to $28,500 in May 2022 before sliding further to $20,000 in June.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph quoted analyst Jeff Ko saying the calendar alone did not cause prior crashes; specific shocks such as the Mt. Gox aftermath, China ICO crackdowns, Fed tightening, and the Terra/FTX collapses were the real drivers.\",\n    \"Jeff Ko said he does not expect a repeat of the 70% to 80% drawdowns from past cycles because spot ETFs, corporate treasury adoption, and the CLARITY Act have broadened the buyer base.\",\n    \"Ko said a move toward the mid-$60k or high-$50k range could be defensible under a macro shock or a significant ETF outflow cascade, but a return to $33,000 would likely require something systemic to break.\",\n    \"Michaël van de Poppe said the current price action does not scream for new lows, but he flagged the $76,000 area as a key support level; losing it could open the door to a deeper decline.\",\n    \"CoinDesk’s feed framed the broader move as Bitcoin sliding under $77,000 as an oil shock and rising Treasury yields pressured risk assets.\",\n    \"Google News surfaced multiple corroborating headlines around the same move, including CoinDesk’s 'Why is bitcoin down? BTC slides to $78,000, SOL, DOGE, XRP down 5%' and Bloomberg’s 'Bitcoin (BTC) Hits Two-Week Low as Crypto Liquidations Top Half a Billion.'\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"CoinDesk RSS headline and summary: 'Bitcoin slides under $77,000 as oil shock and Treasury yields hit risk assets.' The feed summary said long-term holders are still sitting tight and exchange balances remain near six-year lows, according to Binance Research data, but underwater short-term holders leave BTC vulnerable to more downside. CoinDesk also highlighted another related market item: 'Crypto traders betting on a rally lose $563 million in liquidations. Ether and bitcoin suffer the most,' with the summary noting that ether and bitcoin led liquidations as prices dropped on macroeconomic concerns. CoinDesk’s RSS stream for today also included 'Why is bitcoin down? BTC slides to $78,000, SOL, DOGE, XRP down 5%' in Google News aggregation, reinforcing that this was a broad market selloff rather than a single-asset event.\\n\\nCoinTelegraph article text: Crypto analysts are divided over whether markets will see a major Bitcoin sell-off in May, a pattern that has emerged in the last two bear markets during US mid-term election years. In May 2018, Bitcoin crashed from nearly $10,000 to about $7,000 by the end of the month. It happened again in May 2022, when Bitcoin fell nearly 30% from about $40,000 to $28,500 before falling further in June to $20,000. With 2026 also a bear market year coinciding with a US mid-term election, there are concerns it could happen again. Crypto analyst Merlijn Enkelaar said, 'The most brutal pattern in Bitcoin history. Nobody wants to hear this. But the pattern is perfect. Mid-term election years. Bitcoin dumps. Every time.' Enkelaar said a similar move could see Bitcoin prices collapse to $33,000 despite the advancement of key legislation, the CLARITY Act, positive crypto sentiment from the Trump administration and potential trade deals between the US and China. Joao Wedson, founder and CEO of Alphractal, also said there would be a higher probability of a new capitulation phase if Bitcoin remains under $78,000, with bears 'showing signs of strength.' Bitcoin was trading at about $76,900 at the time of writing, down 5.6% over the past seven days.\\n\\nCoinTelegraph analysis continued: Jeff Ko, chief analyst at the CoinEx exchange, told Cointelegraph that midterm election years have coincided with major Bitcoin bear markets, 'so some traders may be tempted to frame 2026 as another “sell in May” setup.' However, behind that historical seasonality were more concrete macro drivers, such as the Mt. Gox aftermath, China’s ICO crackdown, Fed tightening and the Terra/FTX collapses. 'The calendar didn’t cause those drawdowns — specific shocks did.' Ko said he doesn’t expect BTC to repeat the 70% to 80% drawdowns seen in past cycles because the market structure has fundamentally changed. 'Spot ETFs, corporate treasury adoption, and the CLARITY Act moving through Congress have meaningfully broadened and institutionalized the buyer base compared with past cycles,' he added. 'In my view, a move toward the mid-$60k or high-$50k range could be defensible under a macro shock or a significant ETF outflow cascade. But a move back to $33k would likely require something genuinely systemic to break, rather than simply a repeat of historical seasonality.' MN Fund founder Michaël van de Poppe was also bullish, saying the current Bitcoin price action 'doesn’t shout for new lows' but is 'consolidating after a run of 40%.' However, an important support level that is currently preventing a larger decline is the $76,000 area, he cautioned. 'If that level is lost, I would assume that the markets will see a further downward fall towards lower boundaries.'\\n\\nGoogle News aggregation corroboration: one headline from Yahoo Finance read 'Harvard Dumps Its Ethereum and Bitcoin ETF Investment.' Another from CoinDesk repeated the broader move, 'Why is bitcoin down? BTC slides to $78,000, SOL, DOGE, XRP down 5%.' TradingView carried 'Crypto crash: why are Bitcoin Cash, LUNC, Pi Network, WLFI going down?' Crypto Briefing had a related headline, 'Iran launches Hormuz Safe, a Bitcoin-settled maritime insurance platform for Persian Gulf shipping.' The Block surfaced 'US crypto ATM operator Bitcoin Depot files for Chapter 11 to wind down business.' Bloomberg’s title in the feed said 'Bitcoin (BTC) Hits Two-Week Low as Crypto Liquidations Top Half a Billion.'\\n\\nTaken together, the live feeds point to a single dominant event today: a market-wide Bitcoin pullback into the $76k-$78k range, with liquidations, oil-related macro pressure, and weak risk sentiment driving the move. Analysts are debating whether this is just a seasonal 'sell in May' scare or the start of a deeper correction, but the immediate facts are that Bitcoin has slipped below a key support band, crypto liquidations have surged, and multiple outlets are framing the same downside move as the day’s central crypto story.\"\n}\nCHART_COIN: bitcoin"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4-mini","usage":{"input":2340,"output":2372,"cacheRead":31872,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":36584,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1779089515087,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DgmkBzZ3kFmPfE8GhI4t48Zlo7YA2"}}
